Set the ICU default locale from a forwarded switch, on every platform.

Upstream sets it only inside `#if BUILDFLAG(IS_CHROMEOS)`, with a comment explaining
that the ChromeOS zygote starts before the browser and so needs it done there. On
Windows, macOS and Linux it is never set at all, and ICU keeps the system locale for
the life of the renderer.

That produces a realm which contradicts itself. A page reaches its locale through
Blink's per-context language and answers correctly, but anything asking ICU directly
answers with the host's. Measured on a mac whose system locale is Russian, on a
profile claiming pl-PL:

    surface                page                    service worker
    navigator.language     pl-PL                   pl-PL
    Intl timeZone          Europe/Warsaw           Europe/Warsaw
    Intl resolved locale   pl-PL                   ru
    currency name          1,00 dolara ameryka...  1,00 доллара США
    month name             styczeń                 январь

creepjs reports exactly this pairing under ServiceWorkerGlobalScope and marks it
"confidence: high" — one scope answering half in the profile's language and half in
the host's is a stronger signal than either value alone.

Dedicated workers, shared workers, iframes and iframe-workers all probe clean; the
service worker is the only realm that reaches ICU without Blink in front of it.

NOT --lang, and that was measured rather than assumed. The browser RESOLVES --lang
against the UI locales the build actually ships and hands children the resolved value:
on an English-only build, --lang=pl-PL, --lang=de-DE and --lang=ja-JP all arrive in the
renderer as --lang=en-US, and Intl answered en-US for all three. A first version of
this patch read switches::kLang, compiled, ran, and did nothing. Only the control arm
caught it. --fingerprint-language is forwarded verbatim, the way --timezone already is.

The include goes in the FILE's include block, not beside the code that uses it: the
header opens `namespace switches {`, and a first attempt placed it inside
RendererMain(), which fails with "namespaces can only be defined in global or
namespace scope".

--- a/content/renderer/renderer_main.cc
+++ b/content/renderer/renderer_main.cc
@@ -60,2 +60,3 @@
 #include "third_party/webrtc_overrides/init_webrtc.h"  // nogncheck
 #include "ui/base/ui_base_switches.h"
+#include "components/ungoogled/ungoogled_switches.h"
@@ -179,4 +179,30 @@ int RendererMain(MainFunctionParams parameters) {
   chromeos::system::EnableCoreSchedulingIfAvailable();
 #endif  // BUILDFLAG(IS_CHROMEOS)

+#if !BUILDFLAG(IS_CHROMEOS)
+  // Everywhere else, ICU keeps the SYSTEM locale unless told otherwise, and a
+  // service worker asks ICU directly — Blink's per-context language never
+  // reaches it. So a profile could present pl-PL on the page and format
+  // currency in the host's language inside its own worker.
+  //
+  // The assignment is the same one ChromeOS already makes a few lines above;
+  // only its platform fence differs.
+  //
+  // The switch is NOT --lang. The browser resolves that one against the UI
+  // locales it ships and hands children the RESOLVED value — measured, every
+  // language collapses to en-US on an English-only build — so reading it here
+  // produced an assignment that was faithful and useless.
+  // --fingerprint-language is forwarded verbatim, like --timezone.
+  {
+    const base::CommandLine& cmd = *parameters.command_line;
+    if (cmd.HasSwitch(switches::kFingerprintLanguage)) {
+      const std::string locale =
+          cmd.GetSwitchValueASCII(switches::kFingerprintLanguage);
+      if (!locale.empty()) {
+        base::i18n::SetICUDefaultLocale(locale);
+      }
+    }
+  }
+#endif  // !BUILDFLAG(IS_CHROMEOS)
+
   InitializeSkia();

